N('mig','Migraine','Neurology','brain','','Recurrent headache disorder with moderate to severe throbbing pain, often with nausea and sensitivity to light and sound.','Unilateral throbbing headache|Nausea|Photophobia and phonophobia|Aura in some patients','Clinical history~First-line~Diagnosis is clinical using ICHD-3 criteria.~Relies on accurate history;MRI brain~Complication assessment~Reserved for red flags or atypical features.~Usually normal','Recurrent attacks of 4 to 72 hours meeting ICHD-3 criteria with a normal neurological examination.','Tension-type headache — bilateral pressing pain, no nausea|Cluster headache — severe unilateral orbital pain with autonomic features|Subarachnoid haemorrhage — thunderclap onset','Triptans and NSAIDs for acute attacks|Preventives such as propranolol, topiramate or CGRP antagonists|Trigger and lifestyle management','Medication-overuse headache|Status migrainosus','Often improves with age; manageable with treatment.','stroke:Differential (aura);epi:Differential (aura)','ICHD-3 classification|NICE guideline CG150');
N('oa','Osteoarthritis','Musculoskeletal','bone','','Chronic degenerative joint disease with cartilage loss and bone remodelling, most often in knees, hips and hands.','Joint pain worse with use|Brief morning stiffness|Reduced movement|Crepitus','Clinical diagnosis~First-line~Age over 45 with activity-related pain and short stiffness.~Imaging not routinely needed;X-ray~Confirmatory~Shows joint space narrowing and osteophytes.~Poor correlation with symptoms','Clinical in typical presentations; X-ray if atypical.','Rheumatoid arthritis — prolonged stiffness, small joints, raised inflammatory markers|Gout — acute red hot joint, urate crystals','Exercise and weight loss|Topical NSAIDs and analgesia|Joint injections|Joint replacement for severe disease','Disability|Chronic pain','Slowly progressive; joint replacement is effective in advanced disease.','ra:Differential;op:Shared risk factors','NICE guideline NG226|OARSI guidelines');
N('gout','Gout','Musculoskeletal','bone','kidneys','Crystal arthritis caused by monosodium urate deposition in joints with hyperuricaemia.','Sudden severe joint pain|Red hot swollen big toe joint|Tophi in chronic disease','Joint aspiration with polarised microscopy~Gold standard~Needle-shaped negatively birefringent urate crystals.~Needs skilled sampling;Serum urate~Monitoring~Guides urate-lowering therapy.~Can be normal during a flare','Crystal identification, or typical presentation with hyperuricaemia.','Septic arthritis — fever, positive culture, must be excluded|Pseudogout — calcium pyrophosphate crystals, knee or wrist|Cellulitis — no joint effusion','NSAIDs, colchicine or steroids for flares|Allopurinol or febuxostat for prevention|Diet and alcohol moderation','Tophi|Joint damage|Kidney stones','Excellent control with urate-lowering therapy.','ra:Differential;ckd:Shared risk factors;htn:Shared risk factors','ACR gout guideline|EULAR gout recommendations','','High-purine diet|Alcohol|Diuretics|Chronic kidney disease|Obesity');
N('eczema','Atopic Dermatitis (Eczema)','Dermatology','skin','','Chronic relapsing itchy inflammatory skin disease with an impaired skin barrier.','Intense itch|Dry skin|Red patches in flexures|Sleep loss from scratching','Clinical diagnosis~First-line~Itch with typical distribution and relapsing course.~Based on history;Patch testing or skin swabs~Complication assessment~Identify contact allergy or infection.~Selective use','Clinical, using UK Working Party or Hanifin-Rajka criteria.','Psoriasis — well-demarcated scaly plaques on extensor sites|Contact dermatitis — exposure pattern|Scabies — burrows, itch in household contacts','Emollients|Topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors|Dupilumab or JAK inhibitors in severe disease','Skin infection|Sleep disturbance|Eczema herpeticum','Often improves in childhood; may persist in adults.','psor:Differential;asthma:Shared risk factors','NICE guideline CG57|AAD atopic dermatitis guidelines');
N('gad','Generalised Anxiety Disorder','Psychiatry','brain','','Excessive, hard-to-control worry about everyday matters lasting at least 6 months.','Persistent worry|Restlessness|Muscle tension|Poor sleep|Irritability','GAD-7 questionnaire~Screening~Quantifies anxiety severity.~Not diagnostic alone;TSH and full blood count~Complication assessment~Exclude thyroid disease and anaemia.~Indirect','DSM-5 criteria after excluding medical and substance causes.','Hyperthyroidism — weight loss, tremor, abnormal TSH|Panic disorder — discrete attacks|Depression — predominant low mood','Cognitive behavioural therapy|SSRIs or SNRIs|Lifestyle measures','Depression|Substance misuse','Chronic but responds to treatment.','mdd:Shared symptoms;hypo:Differential','NICE guideline CG113|APA anxiety guidance');
N('covid','COVID-19','Infectious','lungs','blood','Respiratory and systemic infection caused by SARS-CoV-2.','Fever|Cough|Loss of smell or taste|Fatigue|Breathlessness in severe disease','RT-PCR or antigen test~Confirmatory~Detects viral RNA or antigen.~Antigen tests are less sensitive;Oxygen saturation~Monitoring~Detects hypoxia.~Can be normal early;Chest X-ray or CT~Complication assessment~Shows pneumonia.~Not needed for mild disease','Positive test with compatible illness.','Influenza — seasonal, similar symptoms, distinguished by testing|Common cold — milder, no systemic features|Bacterial pneumonia — purulent sputum, lobar consolidation','Supportive care|Antivirals such as nirmatrelvir-ritonavir in high-risk patients|Oxygen and dexamethasone in severe disease|Vaccination','Pneumonia and ARDS|Thrombosis|Long COVID','Most recover; risk is higher in older and unvaccinated patients.','pneu:Complication;copd:Complication;asthma:Complication','WHO COVID-19 clinical management guideline|NIH COVID-19 treatment guidelines');
N('vte','Venous Thromboembolism (DVT and PE)','Haematology','blood','lungs','Blood clots in deep veins that can travel to the lungs.','Unilateral leg swelling and pain|Sudden breathlessness|Pleuritic chest pain|Haemoptysis','Wells score and D-dimer~Screening~Low probability with a negative D-dimer needs no imaging.~D-dimer is non-specific;Compression ultrasound~Confirmatory~Detects proximal DVT.~Operator dependent;CT pulmonary angiography~Gold standard~Detects pulmonary embolism.~Radiation and contrast','Imaging-confirmed thrombus after risk stratification.','Myocardial infarction — ECG and troponin changes|Pneumonia — fever and consolidation|Cellulitis — warm red skin without venous thrombosis','Anticoagulation (DOACs or LMWH)|Thrombolysis in massive PE|Compression and mobilisation','Chronic thromboembolic disease|Post-thrombotic syndrome|Death','Good with timely anticoagulation.','mi:Differential (chest pain);pneu:Differential (pleuritic pain)','ESC pulmonary embolism guidelines|NICE guideline NG158','','Surgery|Immobility|Cancer|Pregnancy|Previous VTE|Oestrogen therapy');
N('kstone','Kidney Stones','Renal and urinary','kidneys','bladder','Crystalline stones in the urinary tract, most often calcium oxalate.','Severe colicky flank pain radiating to the groin|Haematuria|Nausea and vomiting','Non-contrast CT KUB~Gold standard~Detects stones and obstruction.~Radiation;Urinalysis~First-line~Haematuria and infection.~Non-specific;Ultrasound~Screening~Useful in pregnancy and children.~Misses small ureteric stones','Typical colic with imaging showing a stone.','Appendicitis — migratory pain, peritonism|Aortic aneurysm — pulsatile mass, older age|Pyelonephritis — fever and pyuria','Analgesia (NSAIDs) and fluids|Medical expulsive therapy for small stones|Lithotripsy or ureteroscopy|Hydration and diet for prevention','Obstruction and infection|Kidney damage','Recurrence is common without prevention.','uti:Complication;ckd:Complication;gout:Shared risk factors','EAU urolithiasis guidelines|AUA stone guideline');
N('gstone','Gallstones','Digestive','liver','pancreas','Stones in the gallbladder, usually cholesterol, often silent.','Biliary colic after fatty meals|Right upper quadrant pain|Nausea|Often asymptomatic','Abdominal ultrasound~First-line~Detects stones and gallbladder wall changes.~Misses common bile duct stones;Liver function tests~Complication assessment~Raised bilirubin and ALP suggest duct obstruction.~Non-specific;MRCP~Confirmatory~Visualises bile duct stones.~Availability','Ultrasound-proven stones with compatible symptoms.','Peptic ulcer — epigastric pain related to meals|GERD — heartburn|Hepatitis — raised transaminases','Laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic stones|ERCP for duct stones|Analgesia','Cholecystitis|Cholangitis|Pancreatitis','Excellent after cholecystectomy.','pjaund:Differential (jaundice);gerd:Differential (upper abdominal pain)','EASL gallstone guidelines|NICE guideline CG188','','Female sex|Age|Obesity|Rapid weight loss|Pregnancy');
N('lungca','Lung Cancer','Oncology','lungs','brain,bone,liver','Malignant tumour of the lung, strongly linked to smoking; non-small cell is most common.','Persistent cough|Haemoptysis|Weight loss|Breathlessness|Chest pain','Chest X-ray and CT~First-line~Detect a mass and stage disease.~X-ray can miss small lesions;Bronchoscopy or CT-guided biopsy~Gold standard~Histology and molecular testing.~Invasive;PET-CT~Staging~Detects spread.~False positives','Tissue diagnosis with staging and molecular profiling.','Tuberculosis — positive cultures, cavitation|Pneumonia — fever and resolution with antibiotics|Pulmonary metastases — known primary cancer','Surgery for early disease|Radiotherapy and chemotherapy|Targeted therapy and immunotherapy','Metastases|SVC obstruction|Paraneoplastic syndromes','Stage dependent; early detection improves survival.','copd:Shared risk factors;tb:Differential (cough, weight loss);pneu:Differential','NCCN NSCLC guidelines|NICE guideline NG122','','Smoking|Radon|Asbestos|Air pollution');
N('crc','Colorectal Cancer','Oncology','intestine','liver','Malignancy of the colon or rectum, usually arising from adenomatous polyps.','Change in bowel habit|Rectal bleeding|Weight loss|Abdominal pain|Iron-deficiency anaemia','Colonoscopy with biopsy~Gold standard~Direct visualisation and histology.~Invasive;Faecal immunochemical test~Screening~Detects occult blood.~Positive result needs colonoscopy;CT staging~Staging~Assesses spread.~Radiation','Histological confirmation at colonoscopy with staging imaging.','Inflammatory bowel disease — younger, chronic diarrhoea|Haemorrhoids — bright red blood on paper, normal endoscopy|Diverticular disease — left-sided pain','Surgical resection|Chemotherapy|Radiotherapy for rectal cancer|Targeted therapy','Obstruction|Metastasis to liver and lungs','Stage dependent; screening improves outcomes.','ibd:Shared risk factors;ida:Complication','NCCN colon cancer guidelines|NICE guideline NG151','','Age|Family history|Lynch syndrome|IBD|Red meat|Obesity');
N('prostate','Prostate Cancer','Oncology','prostate','bone','Common malignancy of the prostate gland, usually adenocarcinoma.','Often no symptoms|Weak urine stream|Frequency and nocturia|Bone pain in metastatic disease','PSA~Screening~Raised levels prompt further assessment.~Raised in BPH and prostatitis;Multiparametric MRI~First-line~Guides biopsy.~Needs expertise;Prostate biopsy~Gold standard~Gleason grading.~Infection and bleeding risk','Biopsy-proven adenocarcinoma with Grade Group.','Benign prostatic hyperplasia — smooth enlarged gland, lower PSA|Prostatitis — tender gland, fever, raised PSA','Active surveillance for low risk|Radical prostatectomy or radiotherapy|Androgen deprivation therapy in advanced disease','Bone metastases|Urinary and sexual dysfunction','Many cases are slow-growing with excellent survival.','uti:Differential (urinary symptoms)','EAU prostate cancer guidelines|NICE guideline NG131');
N('dem','Alzheimer Disease','Neurology','brain','','Progressive neurodegenerative disease with amyloid plaques and tau tangles, the most common dementia.','Short-term memory loss|Word-finding difficulty|Disorientation|Behaviour change','Cognitive tests (MoCA, MMSE)~First-line~Quantify cognitive impairment.~Education bias;MRI brain~Confirmatory~Shows atrophy and excludes other causes.~Atrophy is not specific;Blood tests (B12, TSH)~Complication assessment~Exclude reversible causes.~Indirect','Clinical criteria with supportive imaging or biomarkers after excluding other causes.','Vascular dementia — stepwise decline, cardiovascular risk|Lewy body dementia — hallucinations, parkinsonism|Depression — mood-predominant, memory complaints','Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil)|Memantine|Non-drug support and carer education|Amyloid-targeting antibodies in selected early disease','Dependence|Falls|Aspiration pneumonia','Progressive over years to a decade.','stroke:Shared risk factors;pd:Differential;mdd:Differential','NIA-AA criteria|NICE guideline NG97');
N('hepb','Hepatitis B','Infectious','liver','blood','Viral infection of the liver that can become chronic.','Often no symptoms|Jaundice|Fatigue|Dark urine|Right upper quadrant discomfort','HBsAg and anti-HBc~Screening~Detects current or past infection.~Interpret with full serology;HBV DNA~Monitoring~Measures viral load.~Cost;Liver function tests and elastography~Staging~Assess liver injury and fibrosis.~Non-specific','Positive HBsAg, with HBV DNA and liver assessment to define phase.','Hepatitis A or C — specific serology|Alcohol-related liver disease — history, AST:ALT above 2|Drug-induced liver injury — drug exposure','Vaccination for prevention|Tenofovir or entecavir when indicated|Surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma','Cirrhosis|Hepatocellular carcinoma|Liver failure','Adults usually clear acute infection; chronic infection needs lifelong monitoring.','hiv:Shared risk factors;pjaund:Differential (jaundice)','WHO hepatitis B guidelines|EASL HBV guideline');
N('malaria','Malaria','Infectious','blood','liver,brain','Mosquito-borne infection by Plasmodium parasites that infect red blood cells.','Cyclical fever and chills|Headache|Sweats|Anaemia|Splenomegaly','Thick and thin blood films~Gold standard~Identify parasites and species.~Operator dependent;Rapid antigen test~First-line~Fast point-of-care result.~Lower sensitivity at low parasitaemia;Full blood count~Complication assessment~Anaemia and thrombocytopenia.~Non-specific','Parasites on blood film or positive rapid test with a travel history.','Dengue — rash, severe myalgia, thrombocytopenia|Typhoid — gradual fever, abdominal symptoms|Influenza — respiratory symptoms','Artemisinin-based combination therapy|IV artesunate for severe malaria|Chemoprophylaxis and bed nets for prevention','Cerebral malaria|Severe anaemia|Acute kidney injury','Excellent with prompt treatment; severe falciparum malaria is life-threatening.','ida:Complication (anaemia)','WHO malaria guidelines|CDC malaria treatment guidance');
N('cf','Cystic Fibrosis','Respiratory','lungs','pancreas,intestine','Autosomal recessive disorder from CFTR mutations causing thick secretions in the lungs, pancreas and gut.','Chronic productive cough|Recurrent chest infections|Poor weight gain|Fatty stools|Salty sweat','Sweat chloride test~Gold standard~Chloride above 60 mmol/L supports diagnosis.~Needs expertise;CFTR genetic testing~Confirmatory~Identifies mutations.~Many variants;Newborn screening~Screening~Immunoreactive trypsinogen.~Requires confirmation','Positive screening with confirmatory sweat test or genetics.','Primary ciliary dyskinesia — situs inversus, normal sweat test|Asthma — reversible airflow limitation|Immunodeficiency — recurrent infections with low immunoglobulins','Airway clearance physiotherapy|CFTR modulators (elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor)|Pancreatic enzyme replacement and nutrition','Bronchiectasis|CF-related diabetes|Liver disease','Survival has improved markedly with modulators.','copd:Differential (chronic cough);pneu:Complication','Cystic Fibrosis Foundation guidelines|ECFS standards of care');
N('mg','Myasthenia Gravis','Neurology','muscle','brain','Autoimmune neuromuscular junction disease with fatigable weakness, usually from acetylcholine receptor antibodies.','Drooping eyelids|Double vision|Weakness worse with activity|Difficulty swallowing and speaking','Acetylcholine receptor antibodies~Confirmatory~Present in most generalised cases.~Negative in some patients;Repetitive nerve stimulation or single-fibre EMG~Confirmatory~Shows decremental response.~Specialist test;CT chest~Complication assessment~Looks for thymoma.~Radiation','Fatigable weakness with positive antibodies or electrophysiology.','ALS — no fatigability, upper and lower motor neuron signs|Lambert-Eaton syndrome — improves with exercise, small cell lung cancer link|Thyroid eye disease — proptosis and thyroid abnormalities','Pyridostigmine|Immunosuppression (steroids, azathioprine)|Thymectomy|IVIG or plasma exchange for crisis','Myasthenic crisis|Aspiration|Treatment side effects','Usually well controlled with treatment.','als:Differential (weakness);pd:Differential (weakness)','International consensus guidance for myasthenia gravis');
N('als','Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis','Neurology','muscle,brain','lungs','Progressive degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons causing weakness and wasting.','Progressive limb weakness|Muscle wasting and fasciculations|Slurred speech|Difficulty swallowing','EMG and nerve conduction studies~Confirmatory~Show widespread denervation.~Findings may be late;MRI brain and spine~Complication assessment~Exclude mimics such as myelopathy.~Normal in ALS;Genetic testing~Screening~C9orf72 and SOD1 in familial cases.~Needs counselling','Clinical criteria after excluding mimics.','Cervical myelopathy — sensory signs, MRI abnormality|Myasthenia gravis — fatigable weakness, antibodies|Multifocal motor neuropathy — conduction block','Riluzole and edaravone|Non-invasive ventilation|Multidisciplinary supportive care and nutrition','Respiratory failure|Malnutrition','Median survival is 3 to 5 years from onset, with wide variation.','mg:Differential (weakness);pd:Differential','EAN ALS guideline|NICE guideline NG42');
N('addison','Addison Disease','Endocrine','adrenal','blood','Primary adrenal insufficiency, most often autoimmune, with cortisol and aldosterone deficiency.','Fatigue|Weight loss|Low blood pressure|Salt craving|Skin hyperpigmentation','Morning cortisol and ACTH~First-line~Low cortisol with high ACTH.~Cortisol varies diurnally;Short synacthen test~Gold standard~Failure of cortisol to rise confirms insufficiency.~Needs stimulation;Sodium, potassium, glucose~Complication assessment~Hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia.~Non-specific','Inadequate cortisol response with elevated ACTH, then adrenal antibodies.','Secondary adrenal insufficiency — low ACTH, no hyperpigmentation|Hypothyroidism — raised TSH|Depression — normal cortisol','Lifelong hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone|Sick-day rules and emergency injection|Medical alert identification','Adrenal crisis|Hypoglycaemia','Normal life expectancy with adherence and education.','hypo:Shared symptoms (fatigue);t1dm:Shared risk factors','Endocrine Society primary adrenal insufficiency guideline');
N('sle','Systemic Lupus Erythematosus','Immunology','skin,kidneys','bone,heart,brain','Chronic autoimmune disease with multi-organ inflammation and autoantibodies.','Fatigue|Joint pain|Malar rash|Photosensitivity|Mouth ulcers','Antinuclear antibody~Screening~Sensitive but not specific.~Positive in other conditions;Anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm~Confirmatory~More specific for SLE.~Titres fluctuate;Complement and urinalysis~Monitoring~Low complement and proteinuria signal activity.~Needs serial tests','EULAR/ACR classification criteria with ANA positivity.','Rheumatoid arthritis — erosive disease, anti-CCP|Drug-induced lupus — drug history, anti-histone antibodies|Fibromyalgia — normal serology','Hydroxychloroquine|Corticosteroids|Mycophenolate, azathioprine or belimumab','Lupus nephritis|Cardiovascular disease|Neuropsychiatric lupus','Much improved survival; flares are common.','ra:Differential;ckd:Complication','EULAR SLE recommendations|EULAR/ACR 2019 classification criteria');
N('haemA','Haemophilia A','Haematology','blood','bone','X-linked recessive deficiency of clotting factor VIII.','Spontaneous bleeding into joints and muscles|Prolonged bleeding after injury|Easy bruising','aPTT~First-line~Prolonged with a normal PT.~Non-specific;Factor VIII assay~Confirmatory~Severity is defined by factor level.~Specialist lab;Genetic testing~Confirmatory~Identifies F8 mutation and carriers.~Needs counselling','Low factor VIII activity with prolonged aPTT.','Von Willebrand disease — mucosal bleeding, both sexes|Haemophilia B — factor IX deficiency|Acquired haemophilia — autoantibodies, no family history','Factor VIII replacement or emicizumab|Prophylaxis to prevent joint bleeds|Gene therapy in selected adults','Haemarthrosis and arthropathy|Inhibitor development|Intracranial bleeding','Near-normal life expectancy with prophylaxis.','ida:Differential','WFH guidelines for haemophilia');
